0; Fr 08:30-14:30"},{"n":"Charter","g":"essentials","k":"supermarket","la":39.459615,"lo":-0.339161,"d":2182,"h":"Mo-Su 09:00-21:30"},{"n":"2010 America's Cup","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":39.466667,"lo":-0.308333,"d":1997,"ds":"The 33rd America's Cup between Société Nautique de Genève defending with team Alinghi against Golden Gate Yacht Club, and their racing team BMW Oracle Racing was the subject of extensive court action and litigation, surpassing in acrimony even the controversial 1988 America's Cup.","wpu":"2010 America's Cup","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/USA-17-flying-cropped.jpg/960px-USA-17-flying-cropped.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Assut de l'Or Bridge","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":39.454806,"lo":-0.349639,"d":2852,"ds":"The Assut de l'Or Bridge (Valencian: Pont de l'Assut de l'Or, Spanish: Puente de l'Assut de l'Or) is a white single-pylon cable-stayed bridge in the City of Arts and Sciences in Valencia, Spain, designed by Valencian architect and civil engineer Santiago Calatrava and completed in December 2008. The name l'Assut de l'Or is Valencian for the Dam of the Gold and refers to a dam that was located nearby.","wpu":"Assut de l'Or Bridge","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/%C3%81gora%2C_Ciudad_de_las_Artes_y_las_Ciencias%2C_Valencia%2C_Espa%C3%B1a%2C_2014-06-29%2C_DD_58.jpg/960px-%C3%81gora%2C_Ciudad_de_las_Artes_y_las_Ciencias%2C_Valencia%2C_Espa%C3%B1a%2C_2014-06-29%2C_DD_58.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Museu de les Ciències Príncipe Felipe","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":39.4558,"lo":-0.3515,"d":3031,"ds":"The Museu de les Ciències Príncipe Felipe (Valencian: Museu de les Ciències Príncep Felip, Spanish: Museo de las Ciencias Príncipe Felipe, anglicised as \"Science Museum Príncipe Felipe\") is a science museum in Valencia, Spain. It is part of the City of Arts and Sciences, an architectural complex within the city, and can be found at the end of Luis García Berlanga Street.","wpu":"Museu de les Ciències Príncipe Felipe","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Museo_Pr%C3%ADncipe_Felipe%2C_Ciudad_de_las_Artes_y_las_Ciencias%2C_Valencia%2C_Espa%C3%B1a%2C_2014-06-29%2C_DD_56.JPG/960px-Museo_Pr%C3%ADncipe_Felipe%2C_Ciudad_de_las_Artes_y_las_Ciencias%2C_Valencia%2C_Espa%C3%B1a%2C_2014-06-29%2C_DD_56.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Palau de les Arts Reina Sofía","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":39.457778,"lo":-0.355556,"d":3421,"ds":"Palau de les Arts Reina Sofía (Valencian: Palau de les Arts Reina Sofia; Spanish: Palacio de las Artes Reina Sofía; anglicised as \"Queen Sofía Palace of the Arts\") is an opera house, performing arts centre, and urban landmark designed by Santiago Calatrava to anchor the northwest end of the City of Arts and Sciences in Valencia, Spain.","wpu":"Palau de les Arts Reina Sofía","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9b/El_Palau_de_les_Arts_Reina_Sof%C3%ADa%2C_Valencia_-_Jan_2007.jpg/960px-El_Palau_de_les_Arts_Reina_Sof%C3%ADa%2C_Valencia_-_Jan_2007.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Mercado de Colón","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":39.468889,"lo":-0.368333,"d":4881,"ds":"Mercado de Colón or Valencian: Mercat de Colom (Columbus Market) is a public market located in the city center of Valencia, Spain. It is one of the main works of the Valencian Art Nouveau.","wpu":"Mercado de Colón","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/Mercado_de_Col%C3%B3n%2C_Valencia%2C_Espa%C3%B1a%2C_2014-06-29%2C_DD_09.JPG/960px-Mercado_de_Col%C3%B3n%2C_Valencia%2C_Espa%C3%B1a%2C_2014-06-29%2C_DD_09.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Monument to James I (Valencia)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":39.471886,"lo":-0.370585,"d":5204,"ds":"Jaume I (Spanish: Jaime I) or the Monument to James I is an instance of public art in Valencia, Spain. The monument is topped by an equestrian bronze statue representing James I of Aragon, conqueror of Valencia in 1238 and founder of the Kingdom of Valencia.","wpu":"Monument to James I (Valencia)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Monument_a_Jaume_I_de_Val%C3%A8ncia.JPG/960px-Monument_a_Jaume_I_de_Val%C3%A8ncia.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Plaza de Toros de Valencia","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":39.466667,"lo":-0.37625,"d":5413,"ds":"Plaza de Toros de Valencia, officially Plaça de Bous de València, is a bullring in Valencia, Spain. It was built between 1850 and 1859 in the neoclassical style, inspired by civil Roman architecture such as the Colosseum in Rome or the Arena of Nîmes (France). It was built by the Valencian architect Sebastián Monleón Estellés.","wpu":"Plaza de Toros de Valencia","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/Plaza_de_toros%2C_Valencia%2C_Espa%C3%B1a%2C_2014-06-30%2C_DD_126.JPG/960px-Plaza_de_toros%2C_Valencia%2C_Espa%C3%B1a%2C_2014-06-30%2C_DD_126.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Del Real Palace, Valencia","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":39.48,"lo":-0.368,"d":5504,"ds":"The now demolished Del Real Palace or Royal Palace (in Valencian, Palau del Real; in Spanish, Palacio del Real) was the former residence of the kings of Valencia in the «Cap i Casal» (head and home) of the kingdom, as the city of Valencia was then called. It was on the left bank of the Turia River, where nowadays Jardines del Real are.","wpu":"Del Real Palace, Valencia","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4f/Palaciodelreal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"González Martí National Museum of Ceramics and Decorative Arts","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":39.47269,"lo":-0.37472,"d":5560,"ds":"The National Museum of Ceramics and Decorative Arts González Martí (Spanish: Museo Nacional de Cerámica y de las Artes Suntuarias González Martí) is a museum in Valencia, Spain, devoted to ceramics –with special importance to Valencian ceramics, porcelains and other decorative arts such as textile art, traditional costumes, and furniture.","wpu":"González Martí National Museum of Ceramics and Decorative Arts","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/Valencia%2C_palazzo_de_marchese_di_Dos_Aig%C3%BCes%2C_esterno_01.jpg/960px-Valencia%2C_palazzo_de_marchese_di_Dos_Aig%C3%BCes%2C_esterno_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Archdiocese of Valencia in Spain","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":39.4758,"lo":-0.375,"d":5746,"ds":"The Archdiocese of Valencia (Latin: Archidioecesis Valentinus) is a Latin Church diocese of the Catholic Church located in north-eastern Spain, in the province of Valencia, part of the autonomous community of Valencia. The archdiocese heads the ecclesiastical province of Valencia, with authority over the suffragan dioceses of Ibiza, Majorca, Minorca, Orihuela-Alicante and Segorbe-Castellón.","wpu":"Archdiocese of Valencia in Spain","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Cimbori_almoina_abril_1.jpg/960px-Cimbori_almoina_abril_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Plaça Redona","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":39.4736,"lo":-0.3766,"d":5750,"ds":"La Plaça Redona, also called Plaça del Clot is a plaza located in the El Mercat(ca) section of the Ciutat Vella district of Valencia, Spain. It lies in the middle of the triangle formed by three nearby plazas: La Plaça del Mercat (\"Market Plaza\"), La Plaça de la Reina (\"Plaza of the Queen\"), and La Plaça de l'Ajuntament (\"City Hall Plaza\").","wpu":"Plaça Redona","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Pla%C3%A7a_Redona_Val%C3%A8ncia.JPG/960px-Pla%C3%A7a_Redona_Val%C3%A8ncia.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Palace of the Borgias","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":39.477847,"lo":-0.374639,"d":5837,"ds":"The Palace of Benicarló (officially and in Valencian, Palau de Benicarló, also commonly known as Palau de les Corts Valencianes or Palau dels Borja) is an aristocratic palace of Valencian Gothic and Renaissance styles located in the city of Valencia, Spain. It is now the headquarters of the Valencian Parliament.","wpu":"Palace of the Borgias","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/25.12.2021_in_Valencia._19.jpg/960px-25.12.2021_in_Valencia._19.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Casa de la Ciutat (Valencia)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":39.476583,"lo":-0.375872,"d":5854,"ds":"The Casa de la Ciutat (in Spanish: Casa de la Ciudad), or what is now called the City Hall, was in the place where today the Gardens known as de la Audiencia or more commonly the gardens next to the Palau de la Generalitat. It was therefore the headquarters of the Municipal Council of Valencia.","wpu":"Casa de la Ciutat (Valencia)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Fa%C3%A7ana_de_la_Casa_de_la_Ciutat_de_Val%C3%A8ncia_abans_de_ser_enderrocada.jpg/960px-Fa%C3%A7ana_de_la_Casa_de_la_Ciutat_de_Val%C3%A8ncia_abans_de_ser_enderrocada.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Mercado Central, Valencia","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":39.473558,"lo":-0.378921,"d":5926,"ds":"Mercado Central or Mercat Central (English: Central Market) is a public market located across from the Llotja de la Seda and the Church of Santos Juanes in central Valencia, Spain. It is one of the main works of the Valencian Art Nouveau.","wpu":"Mercado Central, Valencia","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/Mercat_fa%C3%A7ana5.jpg/960px-Mercat_fa%C3%A7ana5.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Public Library of Valencia","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":39.470278,"lo":-0.381667,"d":5992,"ds":"The Public Library of Valencia is the main library of the city of Valencia. As an institution, it was founded in 1838. However, it was not until 1979 when it was relocated to its present location, the main building of the former Hospital for the Poor Innocents.","wpu":"Public Library of Valencia","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/14/Valencia.Hospital_de_los_Pobres_Inocentes.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Torres de Serranos","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":39.479293,"lo":-0.375933,"d":6016,"ds":"The Serrans Gate or Serranos Gate (Valencian: Porta dels Serrans, Valencian: [ˈpɔɾta ðels seˈrans]; Spanish: Puerta de Serranos, [ˈpweɾta ðe seˈranos]), also known as Serrans Towers or Serranos Towers (Valencian: Torres dels Serrans, Valencian: [ˈtorez ðels seˈrans]; Spanish: Torres de Serranos, [ˈtores ðe seˈranos]) is one of the twelve gates that formed part of the ancient city wall, the Christian Wall (Muralla cri.","wpu":"Torres de Serranos","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Puerta_de_los_Serranos%2C_Valencia%2C_Espa%C3%B1a%2C_2014-06-30%2C_DD_86.JPG/960px-Puerta_de_los_Serranos%2C_Valencia%2C_Espa%C3%B1a%2C_2014-06-30%2C_DD_86.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Teatro Princesa","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":39.4753,"lo":-0.3812,"d":6187,"ds":"Teatro Princesa (English: Princess Theatre) was a theater in Valencia, Spain. Actor Lluís Nonell died during the play El diluvio que viene on 30 January 1982 from a sudden cardiac death.","wpu":"Teatro Princesa","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Solar_on_estava_el_teatre_Princesa%2C_Val%C3%A8ncia.JPG/960px-Solar_on_estava_el_teatre_Princesa%2C_Val%C3%A8ncia.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"}],"about":{"ds":"Valencia, known officially in Valencian as València, is the capital and largest city of the Valencian Community and the province of the same name in Spain. It is located on the banks of the Turia, on the east coast of the Iberian Peninsula on the Mediterranean Sea. With a population of 841,558, it is the 3rd most populous city in Spain.","wpu":"Valencia","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Malvarrosa_Beach%2C_Valencia%2C_Spain_%2829812271043%29.jpg/960px-Malvarrosa_Beach%2C_Valencia%2C_Spain_%2829812271043%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}